{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And your skies above your head shall be copper.</b> These curses Moses said on his own initiative, while those at Mount Sinai were said by the Holy One, blessed be He, as their wording indicates. And so it is written (Leviticus 26) ״וְאִם לֹא תִּשְׁמְעוּ לִי … וְאִם תֵּלְכוּ עִמִּי קֶרִי״, while here it says ״בְּקוֹל ה' אֱלֹהֶיךָ … יַדְבֵּק ה' בְּךָ … יַכְּכָה ה'״. Moses lightened his curses by stating them in the singular. And likewise in this curse he lightened: in the first ones it says ״אֶת שְׁמֵיכֶם כַּבַּרְזֶל וְאֶת אַרְצְכֶם כַּנְּחֻשָּׁה״ — that the skies would not sweat, in the manner that iron does not sweat, and consequently there would be drought in the world, while the earth would sweat, in the manner that copper sweats, and it would rot its fruits. But here he says ״שָׁמֶיךָ נְחֹשֶׁת וְאַרְצְךָ בַּרְזֶל״ — that the skies would sweat, even though they would not pour rain; in any case there would not be a drought of utter destruction in the world, and the earth would not sweat, in the manner that iron does not sweat, and the fruits would not rot. In any case it is a curse — whether it is like copper or whether it is like iron, it will not yield fruit, and likewise the skies will not pour rain (Sifra, Leviticus 26)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:48:39.109827212Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}